      "id": 1,
      "text": "White Deer first appeared as Paton, then Whig, before adopting its current name in January 1899 after the nearby White Deer Creek.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Deer%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1908 the community relocated half a mile east to sit beside the railroad, a move that reshaped its future.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        10,
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/white-deer-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A 1965 historical marker on town grounds commemorates White Deer's resilient past, standing as a local landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Deer%2C_Texas"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Polish settlers Henry Czerner and Ben Urbanczyk founded a farming community on White Deer’s northeastern edge in 1909.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://whitedeer.us/history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 1931 fire, the Great Depression, and tornadoes in 1945 and 1951 all helped trigger White Deer's decline.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
